Runtime/market-experiment follow-up retained from this session.

## Current state
Market experiment: `NOT_EXERCISED`. Live Git Town / executable admission evidence was not exercised where policy/runtime did not permit it. These absences must not be represented as PASS or FAIL.

## Goal
Once the technical and buyer hypotheses are sufficiently specified, execute the real experiment and delivery path against exact subjects and retain replayable receipts.

## Required proof
- exact repository/branch/commit subject;
- explicit experiment hypothesis and stop condition;
- real execution rather than documentation-only inference;
- exact CI/runtime receipts where available;
- delivery lineage (branch/PR/merge or explicitly documented alternative);
- negative/failure outcome preservation;
- no retroactive conversion of `NOT_EXERCISED` into success.

## Git Town boundary
If live Git Town execution is part of the chosen workflow, record actual commands/state/stack lineage. If the environment blocks it, record `BLOCKED_POLICY`/`NOT_EXERCISED` and provide a reproducible local verification contract instead of inventing a receipt.

## Acceptance
A replayable exact-subject market/runtime receipt exists, or the blocker is explicitly captured with a local verification entry point. This gate is separate from customer and paid-adoption evidence.

Ownership/operability gate for any hosted Agent Contract Evolution Replay CI product slice.

## Current state
Durable hosted owner: `UNSELECTED`.

## Goal
Select an explicit long-lived service owner and prove the operational responsibilities needed before claiming a hosted product.

## Required decisions
- owning repository/service and accountable maintainer/team;
- deployment target and lifecycle authority;
- data retention and customer evidence boundary;
- authn/authz and secret ownership;
- incident/rollback ownership;
- cost/budget ownership;
- SLO/availability target if a hosted SLA is claimed;
- upgrade/migration policy for evaluator adapters and historical corpus;
- deprecation/export path so evidence remains portable.

## Runtime proof before completion
- real deployment receipt against an exact subject;
- replayable release/admission receipt;
- rollback or failure-path exercise;
- no claim of hosted production readiness from local/synthetic CI alone.

## Acceptance
Owner and runtime are explicit, durable and auditable. Until then, hosted ownership remains `UNSELECTED` and the product stays in `VALIDATE`.

Commercial/adoption gate for Agent Contract Evolution Replay CI.

## Current state
Paid demand: `ABSENT`.

## Goal
Convert validated recurring pain into at least one credible paid pilot or other binding adoption commitment with explicit scope, owner and success criteria.

## Required evidence
A countable adoption receipt should bind:
- qualified buyer/team;
- concrete replay/contract-evolution use case;
- agreed pilot scope and data/security boundary;
- accountable customer-side owner;
- provider-side owner;
- commercial or binding adoption commitment;
- start/decision date and exit criteria;
- measurable success/failure metrics.

## Non-counting evidence
- compliments, stars, GitHub traffic or generic interest;
- internal repository usage;
- synthetic benchmarks alone;
- unpaid exploratory conversation without a binding commitment;
- inferred willingness to pay.

## Acceptance
At least one explicit paid or binding adoption receipt exists and can be audited without exposing unauthorized private customer information. Negative or declined pilot outcomes remain recorded.

Technical `5 / 5` history evidence does not satisfy this gate.

Market-validation gate for Agent Contract Evolution Replay CI.

## Current state
Customer-origin confirmations: `0 / 3`. Repository-generated technical evidence must not be substituted for buyer evidence.

## Goal
Obtain evidence from at least three qualified teams that repeatedly encounter agent/MCP/tool-contract evolution failures that this product can address.

## Interview/evidence contract
For each qualified team capture, with permission and without secrets:
- team/product context and relevant agent/tool integration surface;
- concrete historical contract-change incident(s);
- frequency/recurrence and engineering cost;
- current detection/migration workflow;
- where existing schema diff, CI, tracing or dependency tooling fails;
- whether historical consumer replay would change a real decision;
- required deployment/security/data-boundary constraints;
- named buying/ownership path where available.

## Qualification
A team counts only when the pain is recurring and materially tied to contract evolution/replay, not generic observability or generic API diffing.

## Acceptance
- at least `3 / 3` independently qualified teams;
- evidence stored as claims/receipts with provenance and redaction boundaries;
- contradictions and negative interviews retained rather than discarded;
- no invented demand score;
- roadmap remains `VALIDATE` until the paid/binding-adoption issue is separately satisfied.

This issue must not include private customer data beyond explicitly authorized evidence.
